{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"springy-asocial-amurminnow","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# The overlap step re-sends the same prefix on every pairwise call, and prompt caching alone cannot fix it\n\n## MEASURE FIRST — and the measurement says the obvious fix is a no-op\n\nFrom a real `rebar review-plan` on this repo with `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` on:\n\n    overlap-judge   18 calls   37,560 input tokens   65.0% of the whole review's input\n                    per call:  min 1,971  max 2,189  mean 2,086\n                    cache_read_tokens: 0   (on every single row)\n    overlap_judge.md system prompt: 1,838 chars ~= 459 tokens\n\n**`CACHE_MIN_PREFIX_TOKENS = 4096`** — the minimum prefix the Anthropic cache will write or read.\nThe ENTIRE per-call input (2,086) is HALF that floor, and the stable prefix (the ~459-token system\nprompt, plus the FIRST digest) is far smaller still.\n\n**So simply routing `judge.py` through `prompts.resolve_prompt_cached` (the byte-stable-prefix seam\n`RunnerAgentStep` uses at `workflow/runs.py:245`, story c6e5) WOULD WRITE AND READ NOTHING.** It\nwould look like a caching fix, change no tokens, and leave `cache_read_tokens: 0` exactly as it is.\nThat failure mode is not hypothetical here — ticket 7a79 exists because the codebase warned about\n\"caching requested but ineffective\" on prompts that were below this same floor and never cacheable.\n**Do not implement caching-as-such without first re-measuring the stable prefix against the floor.**\n\n## Where the saving actually is\n\n`judge.py:judge_one` builds, per candidate pair:\n\n    system_prompt = <constant, ~459 tokens>\n    instructions  = f\"FIRST:\\n{_digest_block(first)}\\n\\nSECOND:\\n{_digest_block(second)}\"\n\nAcross a batch, `system_prompt` AND `first` (the query ticket's digest) are IDENTICAL for every\ncall — only `second` varies. With mean 2,086 tokens per call and a ~459-token system prompt, the\nrepeated content dominates: roughly 800 tokens of FIRST plus the system prefix are re-sent 18 times\nto carry ~800 tokens of new SECOND each time.\n\n## Options, to be chosen in the plan review rather than pre-decided here\n\n1. **Batch N candidates into one call.** One call carrying system + FIRST + all N SECONDs instead of\n   N calls each carrying system + FIRST + one SECOND. Order-of-magnitude estimate: ~15.7k tokens vs\n   37.5k, and the resulting prompt would EXCEED the 4,096 floor, so caching becomes possible for\n   subsequent reviews — the two wins compound. **Cost:** the prompt is deliberately written for an\n   ORDERED PAIR (\"FIRST <relation> SECOND\") with strict per-pair precision rules and a\n   `related_distinct` default; batching risks cross-contamination between candidates and could\n   degrade the precision the prompt works hard for. Any batching change must show precision is\n   preserved, not assumed.\n2. **Enlarge and cache the stable prefix.** Only viable if system + FIRST can credibly exceed 4,096\n   tokens, which today it cannot — padding a prompt to reach a cache floor would be absurd. Record\n   as considered and rejected unless the prompt grows for independent reasons.\n3. **Reduce candidates.** The step already surfaces at most 3 suggestions but JUDGED 18 candidates\n   to find them. Tightening BM25F retrieval (`overlap/retrieve.py`) so fewer candidates reach the\n   judge attacks the same total from the other end, and needs no prompt change at all.\n4. **Accept the cost** now that the model class is fixed (see below) and close this as measured.\n\n## Interaction with `afeb` — sequence matters\n\nThose 18 calls currently run on `cfg.model` — the frontier-class default — ONLY because of bug\n`afeb` (hand-built sub-calls bypass the model-class config). Once `afeb` routes `overlap-judge` to\n`standard`, the SAME token volume gets much cheaper. **Model class is the larger and simpler lever;\nthis ticket is the token-volume lever.** They compose, and `afeb` should land first so this work is\nmeasured against the corrected baseline rather than the inflated one.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] the stable-prefix size is MEASURED against `CACHE_MIN_PREFIX_TOKENS` and the result recorded,\n      before any caching change is written — if the prefix is below the floor, caching is not the\n      fix and the ticket says so\n- [ ] whichever option is chosen, the improvement is demonstrated by a BEFORE/AFTER token comparison\n      from `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` on a real run, not by reasoning about the code. Compare input tokens and\n      `cache_read_tokens`, not call counts — call count is a poor cost proxy\n- [ ] if batching is chosen, judgement PRECISION is shown to be preserved: the same candidate set\n      must yield the same relations as the per-pair implementation, or any difference must be\n      explained and accepted\n- [ ] the step remains ADVISORY and fail-safe — `overlap_findings` returns `[]` on any error today\n      and must continue to; a batching bug must never block or fail a review\n- [ ] `cache_read_tokens > 0` is asserted on a repeat run IF and ONLY IF the chosen design actually\n      makes the prefix cacheable; otherwise the ticket explicitly records that caching was not\n      applicable and why\n- [ ] measured against the post-`afeb` baseline, so the saving attributed here is token volume and\n      not the model-class fix\n\n## Notes\n\nIn scope for epic 061c at the operator's direction. Discovered while measuring which calls ignore the\nmodel-class configuration; the token aggregation above is from the same run that produced `afeb`'s\nevidence. Related: `9416` (renaming/documenting this feature), `7a79` (the cache floor).","id":"8415-b6e8-c206-48f6","parent_id":"061c-ecd1-8967-4a76","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Cut the overlap step's token cost: the shared prefix is re-sent 18 times and is too small to cache"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785628872721844001,"uuid":"226abc4e-8208-4de9-b142-9ffa5a1c5bdf"}